the many points in columns 1 and 2, we likely need vertical lines for those columns. For example, we could use vertical lines x=1 and x=2 as the two non-sunny lines. That would cover all points in columns 1 and 2. Then we have columns 3-6 left, with points as above. We have 4 sunny lines left to cover those points. As we reasoned earlier, we need to assign each point in column 3 (4 points) to a distinct sunny line (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column). So each of the 4 sunny lines must take one point from column 3. Then column 4 has 3 points, so three of the sunny lines must take a point from column 4. Column 5 has 2 points, so two of the sunny lines must take a point from column 5. Column 6 has 1 point, so one sunny line must take that point.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines L1-L4 such that:\n\n- L1 passes through (3, y1_3) and maybe (4, y1_4) and maybe (5, y1_5) and maybe (6, y1_6).\n- L2 passes through (3, y2_3) and maybe (4, y2_4) etc.\n- L3 passes through (3, y3_3) ...\n- L4 passes through (3, y4_3) ...\n\nThe y_i_j must be integer and within allowed range.\n\nWe need to assign points such that each line is straight (collinear). So for each line, the points assigned must satisfy a linear relation.\n\nWe can think of constructing a 4x4 matrix of points (x_i, y_i) for each line i and column x_i (3,4,5,6). For each line i, we can assign a subset of columns (maybe not all). But we need to cover all points. So each point must be assigned to at least one line. Since we have exactly 4 lines, and column 3 has 4 points, each line must cover exactly one point from column 3. So each line must have at least one point from column 3. Similarly, column 4 has 3 points, so three lines must have a point from column 4. Column 5 has 2 points, so two lines must have a point from column 5. Column 6 has 1 point, so one line must have a point from column 6.\n\nThus we can think of a pattern: assign points to lines as follows:\n\n- L1: (3, a1), (4, b1), (5, c1),